## Migration

[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Demographics_of_Bulgaria&action=edit&section=21 "Edit section: Migration")]

Main article: [Immigration to Bulgaria](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_to_Bulgaria "Immigration to Bulgaria")

### Historical migration

[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Demographics_of_Bulgaria&action=edit&section=22 "Edit section: Historical migration")]

The first censuses of the [Principality of Bulgaria](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Bulgaria "Principality of Bulgaria") and the autonomous province of [Eastern Rumelia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Rumelia "Eastern Rumelia") in 1880 recorded 31,786 and 17,970 Bulgarian refugees from [Macedonia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_%28region%29 "Macedonia (region)") and [Ottoman Thrace](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adrianople_Vilayet "Adrianople Vilayet"), respectively, who accounted for 1.38% of the population of the Principality an 2.20% of the population of the autonomous province, respectively.[[77]](#cite_note-1881_Census-79)[[78]](#cite_note-Balev-80)[[79]](#cite_note-Thrace-81) The census of the Principality also counted a total of 37,635 people, or 1.88% of the population, born in a country other than the [Ottoman Empire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ottoman_Empire "Ottoman Empire"), mostly Bulgarians from [Romania](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarians_in_Romania "Bulgarians in Romania"), [Northern Dobruja](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Dobruja "Northern Dobruja") and [Bessarabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessarabian_Bulgarians "Bessarabian Bulgarians").[[77]](#cite_note-1881_Census-79) By 1887, when the first joint census of the Principality and the autonomous province was conducted following their peaceful [unification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_unification "Bulgarian unification") in 1885, the number of the refugees from the 

